    <h1>{title}</h1>
    <p class="subtitle">{description}</p>

    <p>Once you've decided solar makes sense for your home, the process from here is fairly straightforward. Here's how to go from "thinking about it" to a working system on your roof.</p>

    <div class="cta-box">
      <p>Ready to take the first step? <a href="https://www.paidonresults.net/c/63227/1/2059/0" target="_blank" rel="noopener sponsored" class="cta-button">Get a Free Solar Quote from Voltacon Solar →</a></p>
    </div>

    <h2>1. Check Your Home's Suitability</h2>
    <p>Before requesting quotes, it helps to have a rough idea of your roof's orientation, shading, and condition. This won't replace a proper survey, but it means you'll understand what installers are telling you.</p>

    <h2>2. Work Out Your Budget and Goals</h2>
    <p>Decide what you're mainly trying to achieve — lower bills, energy independence, or environmental impact — and roughly what you're willing to spend. This helps installers recommend the right system size instead of overselling.</p>

    <h2>3. Get Multiple Quotes</h2>
    <p>Aim for at least 2-3 quotes from different installers. Prices and recommended system sizes can vary more than you'd expect, and comparing quotes side by side makes it easier to spot anything that doesn't add up.</p>

    <h2>4. Check for MCS Certification</h2>
    <p>Only use installers certified by the Microgeneration Certification Scheme (MCS). This is required to qualify for certain government incentives, and it's a strong indicator of a properly vetted, competent installer.</p>

    <h2>5. Ask About the Survey Process</h2>
    <p>A proper installer will carry out a site survey — either in person or remotely using satellite imagery — to confirm roof suitability, shading, and the ideal system size before finalising a quote.</p>

    <h2>6. Understand the Installation Timeline</h2>
    <p>Most residential installations take just 1-2 days once scheduled, though the wait between signing a contract and installation date can range from a few weeks to a couple of months depending on the installer's workload.</p>

    <h2>7. Know What Happens After Installation</h2>
    <p>Once fitted, your installer will register the system, explain how to monitor your output (usually via an app), and provide documentation you'll need for any applicable grants or export payment schemes.</p>

    <h2>The Bottom Line</h2>
    <p>Getting started with solar doesn't need to be complicated. Understand your roof, compare a few quotes from certified installers, and ask the right questions — the rest of the process is largely handled for you.</p>

